The key observation is that we fully control the MT19937 seed and therefore know every 32-bit value used as AES input. From the first known plaintext block, each sample gives us a plaintext/ciphertext pair for the unknown AES permutation on inputs of the form `u || 0^12`. That smells like a reduced-domain codebook attack, but 100 samples per connection is far too small unless there is some way to force the PRNG outputs to be chosen or repeated. To look for a simpler angle, I’ll measure exactly how many ciphertext lines we get for many seeds; the fact we saw 77 instead of 100 may indicate dropped lines due to embedded newlines in ciphertext, which can be exploited because raw ciphertext is being line-delimited.
