2026-10-10 10:15:56,242 INFO 💭 THOUGHT (primary)
DISCUSSION
This is useful: total bytes are always 4900 = 100*(48+1), confirming there are exactly 100 ciphertexts with newline separators and some ciphertexts contain embedded `0x0a`. So every query gives us a noisy oracle on where newline bytes appear in the 48-byte ciphertext stream. Because the first 16 plaintext bytes are known, the event `cipher_byte == 0x0a` at positions 0..15 translates directly into `AES_k(input)_byte == known_plain_byte ^ 0x0a`. That may let us identify AES output bytes statistically for chosen PRNG inputs, but I want a cleaner dataset: raw 4900 bytes preserved exactly, not line-split. I’ll create a script that stores the full response body and parses it by fixed 49-byte records.
COMMAND
create fixedparse.py
